#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bf8bb23-741b-5583-b51c-67eabae0ab54" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "07394b09-ab80-579c-9b10-dc7a2835d3c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6595" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 25y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x}  = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2}  = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+25{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+25) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny = P\\cos{5x} + Q\\sin{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087535 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of a Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "b22fa1be-4c82-5dbf-b5fe-5cbb42c0c646" :question/math-academy-id "e-6596" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(t)$ satisfies the equation $y'' - 4y' + 85y = 0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n- $-2e^{2t}\\cos{9t} + 7e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$\n\n- $e^{2t}\\cos{9t}+3e^{2t}\\sin{3t}$\n\n- $-6e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+85{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 85 = 0,\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+85 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-81 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 9\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\lambda = 2 \\pm 9 \\textrm{i}$ are the complex roots of the characteristic equation, the general solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny = e^{2t} \\left( P \\cos 9t + Q \\sin 9t \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $P=-2$ and $Q=7.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II does *not* take the above form. It contains $\\sin 3t,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III takes the above form with $P=0$ and $Q=-6.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087536 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "191e98e0-aa31-5dd9-a735-6580d29ad287" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Solutions of a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "7aa1ff4a-2263-5c99-97dd-d6fcada8bfc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6597" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y'' + 10y' + 34y  = 0,$ where $y = y(t).$ If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+34{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +34) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 34  = 0,\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +34 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25)-25+34 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +5 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda = - 5 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2999a565-bf64-5246-9fee-54cecc9e79ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "04253a00-f441-58d8-b97e-f7935ae5c7c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6598" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 4 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 20y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x}  = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2}  = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our ODE gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+20{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20 & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bf8bb23-741b-5583-b51c-67eabae0ab54" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83724dda-5b0d-56c3-a257-ae02c95552bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11\\sqrt{11}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11\\sqrt{11}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347249} {:answer/id #uuid "8acbb943-0168-54fb-8744-ea4634f70d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347250} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6447c0-4689-563a-8fdd-56208f9a8adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{11}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{11}x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347251} {:answer/id #uuid "09e32c39-81c5-5b91-aa44-cdde539a6479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347252} {:answer/id #uuid "70266890-f37f-5216-9f72-e4a3293d3823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{11}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{11}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70266890-f37f-5216-9f72-e4a3293d3823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{11}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{11}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347253} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ab0b5d9-a849-569f-bc42-1182a5daed25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ac0fc10-69b8-518b-a3f6-edf675029517" :question/math-academy-id "q-130153" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+11y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+11{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+11) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+11=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe auxiliary equation has pure imaginary roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+11 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-11 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{11}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{11}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bf8bb23-741b-5583-b51c-67eabae0ab54" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92de3964-d7e4-588e-bc3f-0dd2d853c4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\sqrt{3}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347256} {:answer/id #uuid "8dca77ea-6d5d-57a0-ae89-6aa44f5ca238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347257} {:answer/id #uuid "84659441-44fa-5813-9b00-6bf955b67847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347258} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d68983-7f1e-51ac-b62d-0097761fa5b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347259} {:answer/id #uuid "02d893cb-b711-5028-8eba-85393bde49bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84659441-44fa-5813-9b00-6bf955b67847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347258} :answer-field/id #uuid "521be9d4-1cef-5e12-b31c-7fbfe2ac7993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cac2aeae-98d6-5905-aa8d-5c146b6bfb75" :question/math-academy-id "q-130155" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+3{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe auxiliary equation has pure imaginary roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-3 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "191e98e0-aa31-5dd9-a735-6580d29ad287" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Solutions of a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59202476-aad7-5329-b9d6-857e81f4d37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pm 10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347263} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c9beba-e160-59c4-b7e4-6c07ddb3ef68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347264} {:answer/id #uuid "10d9b684-aecd-5b01-8fd5-f073aa4ae118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347265} {:answer/id #uuid "40c42624-fab1-5b4e-a631-94f250b947c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347266} {:answer/id #uuid "cae6e323-3422-5ca6-82c6-95937d9871a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\pm 3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59202476-aad7-5329-b9d6-857e81f4d37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pm 10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347263} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9a525e3-4dce-5179-ae10-9657f31b095c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97069a00-838c-503e-8cc1-9576f402268b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130164" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+109y=0,$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+109{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +109) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +109=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +109 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+109 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-100 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 10\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 10\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =-3\\pm 10\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2999a565-bf64-5246-9fee-54cecc9e79ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b49e6fca-d7b6-5424-af13-92e5e183a057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347270} {:answer/id #uuid "13223bb4-5cc3-543c-962d-ebb17fbc791f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347271} {:answer/id #uuid "1f701bf7-4a63-560e-a6bf-61e1d52e9163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347272} {:answer/id #uuid "21768ea2-cad9-591e-9065-af66e78844f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-\\sqrt{2}x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347273} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf83363-84fc-54ba-b103-d6eb0fc53cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-\\sqrt{2}x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b49e6fca-d7b6-5424-af13-92e5e183a057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347270} :answer-field/id #uuid "587ceab4-203c-5e3e-89a6-e79e14fc0085" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c02a646-368e-54d5-8b44-55a70a155c58" :question/math-academy-id "q-130166" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+18y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+18{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +18) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +18=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +18 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+18 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2999a565-bf64-5246-9fee-54cecc9e79ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60a816ee-349d-5d90-ae4f-bab8437d14ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{10x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347277} {:answer/id #uuid "80b22cca-2551-5598-8ee8-02c08c8f76de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{10x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347278} {:answer/id #uuid "0932d06b-ddf6-5a06-84f6-0d97052f23ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347279} {:answer/id #uuid "e76a883f-0f76-50c5-b03d-9b9415908410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-10x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347280} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f3648e-ccc1-5411-bd6b-f2dd691fb6c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0932d06b-ddf6-5a06-84f6-0d97052f23ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347279} :answer-field/id #uuid "73ec17ea-3870-591f-af0d-a3c990fe2231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63b867aa-cee8-5481-85ff-5e879b9d9821" :question/math-academy-id "q-130327" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+109y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+109{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +109) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +109=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +109 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+109 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-100 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 10\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 10\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of a Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d677b3c7-fe6f-5d82-86e9-a0bebecd02a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347284} {:answer/id #uuid "4140ac64-3940-5efe-8ec2-71ab8a77b432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347285} {:answer/id #uuid "b34084c6-bdc4-5670-a829-1731b4b7b235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347286} {:answer/id #uuid "40b9d118-62ca-5f90-bb95-34f158533c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x-2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347287} {:answer/id #uuid "fba5e3e8-052e-5cd3-ac56-eb8e822c8d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34084c6-bdc4-5670-a829-1731b4b7b235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347286} :answer-field/id #uuid "8afba1a9-7b8b-5ff3-a33a-ad48325c4b1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d21543bf-bb06-541c-a58e-8a0367707d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130330" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+50y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+50{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +50) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +50=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +50 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+50 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+49 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-49 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 7\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 7\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =-1\\pm 7\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(-1+7\\text{i})x})={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(-1+7\\text{i})x})={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $50{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of a Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bf87abe-9d5a-5383-b473-2af663f38bfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347291} {:answer/id #uuid "c864e5a9-6ac3-5e5f-8ae4-386ed10351cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347292} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca9e62d-0af7-5c29-9226-eb356e9afeef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347293} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebd7184-cb3b-509b-b2cd-8527807e152e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347294} {:answer/id #uuid "1221dd3c-a862-5432-bae7-0238cf18ca17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ca9e62d-0af7-5c29-9226-eb356e9afeef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347293} :answer-field/id #uuid "3702d517-03e9-51ad-91a2-bb0914344831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af9af2fb-ef1d-5340-9a94-3dfeb8f038e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-130335" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}$ are the purely imaginary roots of the auxiliary equation. \n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of a Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b00595dc-961b-598a-a7e6-4f92d750d335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347298} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd674c1-7e58-5df5-8724-dade248e1abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347299} {:answer/id #uuid "57cc03b7-229a-5822-afca-9e9d042e21d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347300} {:answer/id #uuid "0661a131-9355-5bf6-b326-5be225efd2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347301} {:answer/id #uuid "5199578a-42f1-512b-8325-66389d799f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0661a131-9355-5bf6-b326-5be225efd2ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347301} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e08d00b-fa0e-5a02-af64-ebee6d207146" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48df2853-dfc5-51a7-b6a3-b25bc596c153" :question/math-academy-id "q-130336" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+17y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+17{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +17) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +17=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +17 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+17 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =-4\\pm \\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(-4+\\text{i})x})={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(-4+\\text{i})x})={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4{e}^{x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "191e98e0-aa31-5dd9-a735-6580d29ad287" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Solutions of a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a647d8e4-f1fc-5a09-b1c2-bada25e86faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 ± \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085988} {:answer/id #uuid "aeba4de2-1ea5-5793-85a3-71a0db65aaac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085989} {:answer/id #uuid "06574644-cc51-525d-8674-864381218855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085990} {:answer/id #uuid "7733575a-c92c-537e-9a40-623990375fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 ± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085991} {:answer/id #uuid "7a44f641-a446-593c-a903-d45cca5d69c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 ± \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a647d8e4-f1fc-5a09-b1c2-bada25e86faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 ± \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085988} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f82137-fd17-5dff-a7a4-24b5296fb670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70c30108-c302-53ad-8b6c-df208afe3a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-64663" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+10y=0$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+10{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =3\\pm \\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "191e98e0-aa31-5dd9-a735-6580d29ad287" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Solutions of a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3c39489-5f70-52b0-a3bb-f8679260a4d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085994} {:answer/id #uuid "0b299564-376b-5764-b11a-e288a2c93f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6 ± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085995} {:answer/id #uuid "22a3dfed-f85a-5269-b92f-40f605f34ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 6\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085996} {:answer/id #uuid "3731a47e-0b13-5900-942b-bf2d72bcca38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3 ± 10\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085997} {:answer/id #uuid "39cc84c2-384e-5d6d-ac1c-43f81ad1cf10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 ± 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b299564-376b-5764-b11a-e288a2c93f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6 ± 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085995} :answer-field/id #uuid "226f305a-161e-5892-930e-056733b56402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe76a521-0e56-59fe-b5f8-9a0bbdd46826" :question/math-academy-id "q-64694" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+40y=0,$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+40{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40=0\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +40 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36)-36+40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +6 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-6\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =-6\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of a Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95098154-8f31-53ab-a102-dfe883e218ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347305} {:answer/id #uuid "abe76ce1-2c80-57bd-bc3f-312fe1453871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347306} {:answer/id #uuid "81e843c0-2278-5088-8eba-d4a2a4dbc88d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347307} {:answer/id #uuid "c61a220f-9eca-5631-8111-7374e0165e2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347308} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8d1915-a7fd-5489-8914-243896b047b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c61a220f-9eca-5631-8111-7374e0165e2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347308} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bc56112-e2d2-5ed1-921b-6b14da3226b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd53d7ed-c933-5340-a31b-528b4220b307" :question/math-academy-id "q-64698" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+64y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+64{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+64) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+64=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-64 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 8\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda =\\pm 8\\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation. \n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{8\\text{i}t})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{8\\text{i}t})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bf8bb23-741b-5583-b51c-67eabae0ab54" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ac6e35b-874e-5cc9-b440-8b444df7cb58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = P\\cos 2x + Q\\sin 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086701} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc3820e-01a2-527b-ab85-7c7e1f2517c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = P\\cos 8x + Q\\sin 8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086702} {:answer/id #uuid "285ee0cc-555c-5e59-bc04-d7d03e7965ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = P\\cos 4x + Q\\sin 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086703} {:answer/id #uuid "626ac558-86e2-5da0-aeb6-8b0449aa61b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{x}(P\\cos 4x + Q\\sin 4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086704} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8f9141-ea46-5106-a450-1815089c0c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{x}(P\\cos 2x + Q\\sin 2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "285ee0cc-555c-5e59-bc04-d7d03e7965ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = P\\cos 4x + Q\\sin 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086703} :answer-field/id #uuid "be6e2a29-eec9-5f43-a064-5fcccb7e70ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86740d1a-9e3f-5fde-bf6f-bd26506cd57b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7816" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+16=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe auxiliary equation has pure imaginary roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2999a565-bf64-5246-9fee-54cecc9e79ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8290a8f7-cf12-5d79-9d20-e155e01499fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-x}(P\\cos 3x + Q\\sin 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086713} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e579cc-91ac-5ed8-9489-db51cd76604b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-2x}(P\\cos 3x + Q\\sin 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086714} {:answer/id #uuid "02038eb3-392b-59b8-87e8-f1c92558b3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-x}(P\\cos 5x + Q\\sin 5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086715} {:answer/id #uuid "55dcbc4a-d7dd-5277-aa0a-b30986fd56c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{x}(P\\cos 5x + Q\\sin 5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086716} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdca935-1dd0-518e-a6ed-564c0f352b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-2x}(P\\cos 5x + Q\\sin 5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02038eb3-392b-59b8-87e8-f1c92558b3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-x}(P\\cos 5x + Q\\sin 5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086715} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec7d3fd-2d2f-5d57-9113-9af5c6cfd797" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd2157c6-44ec-5848-a33c-3c6d00cdf590" :question/math-academy-id "q-7818" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+26y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+26{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+26 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2999a565-bf64-5246-9fee-54cecc9e79ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00189956-0883-5430-badc-c2c1a84888c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347312} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f40ac4-52f7-58da-bf72-da744f7b8f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347313} {:answer/id #uuid "13d3de89-d893-53eb-80f6-565a2b1c671f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347314} {:answer/id #uuid "182e68d9-3024-58ff-8c2e-0eb7609f4776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347315} {:answer/id #uuid "99936e1f-0329-5cfe-b49b-a23612a804b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99936e1f-0329-5cfe-b49b-a23612a804b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347316} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3c01883-257e-54df-a0fc-7e79878ee753" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d69d8599-b6c0-53a9-9f35-3bea9d1bec0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7819" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+13y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+13{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +13) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +13=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +13 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+13 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bf8bb23-741b-5583-b51c-67eabae0ab54" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a51c1ab7-25db-5e41-8a3d-46eb094465d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347319} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ec1f66-c3ba-5520-aa6e-2c7d9fc5029f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347320} {:answer/id #uuid "537c3cbe-d61a-53bc-ae41-9fd3ab4e9861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347321} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8a8c3c-65f1-5161-8721-c4759766ebc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347322} {:answer/id #uuid "045eb8ac-cb68-53c8-9cd2-a9f113e0a7ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5ec1f66-c3ba-5520-aa6e-2c7d9fc5029f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347320} :answer-field/id #uuid "98172f93-1476-5b7e-9a4e-8d513aaf89bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51ec48c3-c8fe-5439-9c0f-70a1c419805c" :question/math-academy-id "q-90791" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=0,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=7.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+49{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+49=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation has pure imaginary roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-49 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 7\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=7.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7P\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x+7Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=7$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n7Q=7.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-2$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bf8bb23-741b-5583-b51c-67eabae0ab54" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Imaginary Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7843e8ce-9abf-53d0-9929-2e378a91edc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347326} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7e2965-61ac-5680-b703-0a7b2b1cabdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347327} {:answer/id #uuid "76900187-d12a-5e50-aa46-b765cb8a5881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347328} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe7e802-a24e-5f34-a949-d53d2f8b7709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347329} {:answer/id #uuid "0e559aac-bcef-5150-abb6-d86561fbd26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e559aac-bcef-5150-abb6-d86561fbd26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347330} :answer-field/id #uuid "45cd4293-895d-5e0e-b70a-39373cec5441" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4820ea55-118f-5bdc-96e7-abfb76af12b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-90793" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+100y=0,\\,y(0)=5,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=20.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+100{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+100) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+100=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation has pure imaginary roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-100 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 10\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=5.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=20.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-10P\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+10Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=20$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n10Q=20.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=5$ and $Q=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "191e98e0-aa31-5dd9-a735-6580d29ad287" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Solutions of a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707b4973-a4d4-5d05-8a5f-1dcad90207eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347333} {:answer/id #uuid "78337f75-a73a-5716-a9b7-e31ea8a2ec9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347334} {:answer/id #uuid "cbdd4a44-5838-5c87-9f85-7339e4156bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347335} {:answer/id #uuid "98a6dc2e-c80a-5c9d-a959-dff81a39d659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347336} {:answer/id #uuid "376ae6c9-3f82-5515-ab04-08c3848be71c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98a6dc2e-c80a-5c9d-a959-dff81a39d659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347336} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df23489-71b4-5ad0-b9f7-9d102efe8254" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa6c5a43-700b-58fe-9c09-aaa67bfd669a" :question/math-academy-id "q-90797" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+8{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "191e98e0-aa31-5dd9-a735-6580d29ad287" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complex Solutions of a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31eb0a03-e5d0-55a8-bf26-b43dac27c420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347340} {:answer/id #uuid "f43e1fcd-e457-5dcb-b57e-95e0864405d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347341} {:answer/id #uuid "c99cbace-5165-5f66-8a79-2f253bfc7b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\pm 3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347342} {:answer/id #uuid "7b93331b-2305-5ad2-abd5-8d322f86cc44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347343} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c27fa2-47b3-5025-bb39-6a375cc37c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31eb0a03-e5d0-55a8-bf26-b43dac27c420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347340} :answer-field/id #uuid "260377e2-4c00-52c2-8868-485a96161dcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b688cd4-43ae-5206-a6c7-4a874e489c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-90799" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+3y=0,$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+3{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-3 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2999a565-bf64-5246-9fee-54cecc9e79ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution When the Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ddddc7-b901-51ad-b54a-d33ae87d3e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347347} {:answer/id #uuid "1e76a656-4872-5eb7-a62f-eb218492341f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347348} {:answer/id #uuid "d99076a4-14eb-5ece-99d3-91290f3d87df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347349} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3bbc81-1fb1-5ed1-a927-44c9967b450e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347350} {:answer/id #uuid "b3561c27-7095-5ce9-9abf-bf1ff12419ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2ddddc7-b901-51ad-b54a-d33ae87d3e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347347} :answer-field/id #uuid "430f270b-181a-50bd-8766-9d93fae3e7b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ee9204f-cbf7-5f8e-aa10-c66f362b4cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-90802" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+8{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "092a6c8c-7bea-5c53-88ac-f5dc8b16c357" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of a Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5907155d-3351-5525-8d1d-4ad2fb69ecf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347354} {:answer/id #uuid "add85793-6d23-5282-89c7-86a95f8bbd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347355} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbdc9d8-130f-53bb-ac6b-7e6bb0cb84e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347356} {:answer/id #uuid "29d2b3b0-e7d7-5c6a-8b16-e19b7584117a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347357} {:answer/id #uuid "68c8dfac-316b-5387-9deb-cf23ca987b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bbdc9d8-130f-53bb-ac6b-7e6bb0cb84e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347356} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7e5570-66be-50e3-8e45-552f173a26f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d12a760d-b095-5ba3-95f1-2e581862de00" :question/math-academy-id "q-90807" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-12{y}^{′}+40y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+40{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36)-36+40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -6 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =6\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =6\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution."}]}
